<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arthur Conolly</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1807">1807</a>–<a href="/years/1842">1842</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arthur Conolly was a British intelligence officer and traveller in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s army who is credited with coining the phrase 'the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-great-game">Great Game</a>' for the Anglo-Russian rivalry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. In 1841 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> to try to free his colleague <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-stoddart">Charles Stoddart</a>, but was himself imprisoned, and both men were beheaded on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> Nasrullah in June 1842.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arthur-conolly.jpg" alt="Arthur Conolly" width="320" height="351" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Atkinson (1780-1852) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ArthurConolly.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
